Z||000003||b lo 0||03-21-2001||07:24 PM||||Fuel filter-new<BR>Fuel pump-new<BR>Spark plugs-new<BR>ICM-new<BR>Oil press. sensor-new<BR>ECM-new (and NOT cheap)<BR>Relay-not new but checked out OK.<P>Thanks again-you guys respond so quick whenever I have a problem.  I guess there's nothing left but the fuel pressure sensor, so I'll hit that next.  Happy Fiero-ing!  (while I walk-haha! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||03-22-2001||04:22 AM||||Fuel preasure sensor? Thats a new one to me! Where would that be? Are you talking about the fuel regulator? Have you put a fuel preasure gauge on the fuel rail to positively identify the pump problem? If so what readings are you getting?<P>For that matter what was the first thread on this problem? What are the symptoms? From what Im reading it just doesn't sound like a fuel problem but more like a weak coil. If you have a fellow Fiero GT nearby switch out the coils and see if it helps. Cheap way to test components! When was the last time you rebuilt your distributor (pick up coil and module)?<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000005||b lo 0||03-22-2001||07:43 AM||||Well, the car blows pump fuses after it's warm if I go wot from stop, the fuel pump doesn't even run half the time (these are fuel system related problems) and the motor won't rev past 4k (ignition related).  I have been informed by Dad (resident mechanic) that the filter is NOT new, I was thinking of the strainer on the pump.  I will check out the filter next.  WKDFIERO, I rebuilt the distributor about 6 months ago, the car wouldn't start when I bought it, and that's what fixed it.  I appreciate that you have such a high view of my financial standings that you think I could be walking through my garage and stub my toe and say "Where did this OTHER Fiero GT come from?  Oh, well, I guess it's mine."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Thanks for the expertise from a longtime member!||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||03-22-2001||01:40 PM||||Oh no! <P>I didn't mean that you had another GT I meant if you had a friend that had a GT. Down here its not unusual for me to get a call (sometimes Ive made the call) from a fellow Fiero Freak and get asked to help out by borrowing a coil or sensor. Its a way of putting a known working part and seeing if it helps the situation. I carry a spare coil and EGR valve that I know works in my tool bag now and the coil has come to the rescue at least once!<P>Popping fuel pump fuses! Oh man! Ive never seen a Fiero do that! Please let us know what you eventually find out!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gold-86SE||03-22-2001||03:49 PM||||<BR>b lo 0, have you checked the fuel pump plug?  The plug wire for the fuel pump, runs behind the heat shield bolted to the fire wall.  Maybe the plug has a bad connection or a chaffed wire, which lends me to believe that this may be causing the fuse to blow.<P>You may have pinched a wire when you put the tank back under the Fiero, consider pulling it down to verify.<P>Good Luck.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Not So Fair Weather Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||b lo 0||03-25-2001||07:11 PM||||Alright, here goes... filter replaced, no more fuse blowing (REALLY clogged).  However, ignition is no longer getting spark when the ICM is plugged in.  It's a new module, and the problem has been traced to either bad dist. ground or bad wiring (probably the cause of the rev limiting too).  Yes, the GT sits in the driveway (I limped it home Friday) waiting to be fixed.  Oh, poo, I guess I have to drive the Jeep.  Thanx for your help, guys!!!||||reg||1
Z||000009||fyerstarter||03-25-2001||09:34 PM||||WDFIERO:  You can take a coil or ignition module to Autozone and they will test it for you.  For those of us who don't know another soul within a hundred miles with a fiero :P<P>B lo 0:  Sorry I have no words of wisdom for you but I wish you best of luck.||||reg||1
Z||000010||87GTZ34||03-25-2001||11:11 PM||||You may want to ohm out the connector between the dist and coil.  This is from the voice of experience.  My car would intermittantly skip and stall.  It turns out that one of the wires in that short harness, had an internal break in it.  The wire cover was not pierced, broken or open in any way but the wire certainly was.  It does sound like you have/had a couple of different things going on from age and neglect though.||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||03-26-2001||01:43 AM||||Ah, Autozone. Yet another option! <P>Yet in the event of when my coil went out having a spare did save my butt from the hook! And in my club, we have had to rebuild a distributer in the parking lot before a trip caravan. Spare parts saves time!<P>What I usually do is just keep the coil module or p/u coil that I am replacing for preventive maintenance (I know they were still working) and just put them into a little bag. They are small enough not to take up any real space. Our club travels quite a bit and having those little parts is just a little insurance for me and whoever is with us on the side of the road! <P>It helps to travel with mechanics! ||||reg||14
